  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nThis afternoon temperatures are well into the 80s across the\narea, with some locations in Kansas and southeastern sections\nexceeding 90 degrees. Temperatures are near their max, but\nrecords at Grand Island and Hastings are going to be close. The\ncurrent record for the Max temperature today is 90 degrees at\nboth locations. As of 2pm - Grand Island and Hastings have hit \n88 and 86 respectively. \n\nThrough Monday above normal temperatures are the expected across\nthe area. Highs in the upper 80s to low 90s are expected.\nNormal max temperatures for mid October are in the upper 60s.\nSoutherly winds will persist into Sunday as a stationary front\nremains to the north of the area in central Nebraska. The\nstationary front will sag southward on Monday and that brings a\nbit of uncertainty on exact reach of the well above normal \ntemps, but overall outcome is above normal temps. Later Monday, \nthe front will push southward as the main upper trough moves \neastward and pushes the cooler airmass further south. Once this \ntrough and associated disturbances move into the area, the upper\ntrough stalls and max temperatures closer to normal are \nexpected for the remainder of the forecast period. \n\nPrecipitation chances increase as this upper trough approaches\nfrom the west. While there are chances for precipitation on\nMonday, the better chance will be between Tuesday and Thursday.\nThere are discrepancies among the the guidance on how this upper\nlow to our west will evolve, with some solutions showing the low\nbecoming cut off, and others having it being picked up the main\nflow and traversing over the area. This will impact the timing\nof any precipitation. The best location for precipitation will\nbe closer to the front, which is expected to set up across\nKansas. This gives our north central Kansas counties and\nlocations along and south of I-80 the best chance for\nprecipitation, with the most southern locations having the best\nchance for amounts of 1-3 inches. Similar to the precipitation\nevent that occurred near the start of the month, there will\nlikely be bands of higher precipitation and identifying exactly\nwhere those will set up, if even in our area, will be difficult\nand something to watch.",
